From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 02:31:39 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Full BCn decode and Book of the Dead steady-state retention
 fix

Trim was gated on census_id_ < DYN_CENSUS_SLOTS, so allocator behaviour
depended on whether an object fit a diagnostic table. With the table at
512 it only saw tiny constant buffers and freed nothing. Ungating it,
plus raising the table to 8192, drops DynamicBuffer retention from
1029MB / 7098 allocations to 145MB / 1003.

Measured over two long runs: steady state 2791-3282MB (mean 3015) vs a
prior 4034MB pinned just under the 4096MB jetsam limit. Trim regret 0.
Peak still reaches 3896MB, so the transient is improved but not solved.

Trim releases only entries with will_free_at <= coherent_seq_id and
stops at the first fenced entry; a blanket depth cap would be a
use-after-free.

BCn: real BC1/BC2/BC3/BC4/BC5/BC7 decoders (BC7 all 8 modes, tables
verified offline against the 128-bit-per-mode invariant). Undecodable
formats now get deterministic fills instead of raw compressed bytes
uploaded as uncompressed texels, which was an out-of-bounds staging
read. BC6H is still a synthetic fill.

Also behavioural for rendering: configurable BC mip clamp (mipClampBC),
with the logical descriptor preserved so GetDesc does not lie.

Instrumentation: owner census reconciled against Metal
currentAllocatedSize, labelled creation sites, live-object counters,
per-instance FIFO stats, trim regret, sequence-triggered reporting.

Madeira-commit: d6bd546dc685189f4434f87fd15ea7b21009e64f
Rebased: resolved
Class: madeira-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-09-24-dxmt-latest-rebase.md
Offered-upstream: no
